1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,605
Most planners ask which route is fastest.

2
00:00:02,605 --> 00:00:06,149
ShadeRoute also asks where direct sun may fall when you travel.

3
00:00:06,949 --> 00:00:11,380
It compares journey time with modelled clear-sky exposure before you set off.

4
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:15,073
Here is the journey and the shade through the day.

5
00:00:15,873 --> 00:00:19,681
Similar walks cross different street canyons and open spaces.

6
00:00:19,681 --> 00:00:31,104
UKHSA identifies older adults, young children, pregnant women, people on certain medicines, and those with heart, lung, kidney or mobility conditions as higher risk in hot weather.

7
00:00:31,104 --> 00:00:34,489
It may also help carers and outdoor workers.

8
00:00:34,489 --> 00:00:37,450
This is planning support, not medical advice.

9
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:43,075
The US National Weather Service heat index assumes shade and light wind.

10
00:00:43,075 --> 00:00:48,704
Strong sun can add up to about eight degrees Celsius to that apparent value.

11
00:00:48,704 --> 00:00:54,333
Twenty-eight to thirty-six is an upper-bound example, not an air-temperature rise or fixed conversion.

12
00:00:54,333 --> 00:00:58,756
Shade reduces radiant heat; water, rest and cool recovery still matter.

13
00:00:59,556 --> 00:01:07,445
Enter locations, set the London departure time and journey needs, then compare up to three alternatives.

14
00:01:07,445 --> 00:01:13,362
Each shows time, potential direct sun, sensitivity, coverage and known access information.

15
00:01:13,362 --> 00:01:15,334
Unknown evidence stays visible.

16
00:01:16,134 --> 00:01:18,631
Use fullscreen, fit and zoom.

17
00:01:18,631 --> 00:01:23,626
Switch between overhead and three-dimensional views; rotate, tilt and reset.

18
00:01:23,626 --> 00:01:29,619
Keep the route fixed and move from sunrise through midday into evening.

19
00:01:29,619 --> 00:01:37,111
Shadows move while certain shade, possible shade, unknown coverage and the two-hundred-and-fifty-metre limit remain visible.

20
00:01:37,911 --> 00:01:45,288
The pilot can show selected water points, GLA cool spaces, toilets and benches where data exists.

21
00:01:45,288 --> 00:01:49,900
Public buildings, medical help and shade-gap analysis are future possibilities.

22
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:56,816
A prepared offline pack keeps the bundled map and model available; live services remain online.

23
00:01:56,816 --> 00:01:59,122
This is not emergency navigation.

24
00:01:59,922 --> 00:02:07,232
Next is a controlled field pilot in two London hospital corridors, tested with carers and outdoor workers and calibrated against observations.

25
00:02:07,232 --> 00:02:10,364
Stronger claims should follow only if evidence supports them.

26
00:02:10,364 --> 00:02:13,845
Show what the model knows, and what it does not.
